What changed for hub admins
===========================

Only the differences from a hand-run PtokaX. README.systemd covers the unit.


Running
-------

PtokaX runs in the foreground. -d and -p are gone, along with the pid file and
the old init scripts. systemd supervises it instead.

A hub is a systemd instance named after its HubName. A hub called PXdev is
ptokax@PXdev.

	systemctl start|stop|restart ptokax@PXdev
	systemctl stop ptokax.target		every hub on the host

SIGHUP used to terminate. It now restarts the scripts, and nothing else, so
they can be reloaded without opening a client.

	pxctl reload-scripts PXdev

Same code path as ScriptMan.Restart(), so the same effects: script state is
lost and bots re-announce. No disconnects.


Where the files went
--------------------

	/var/lib/ptokax/HubName/	cfg/ scripts/ texts/
	/usr/share/ptokax/language/	shared, a per-hub language/ still wins
	/etc/ptokax/HubName.conf	optional, sets PTOKAX_ARGS

systemd creates the tree, so it is not there until create or import has run.

	sudo pxctl get PXdev statedir

By default the tree sits under /var/lib/private at 0700 root and needs sudo to
reach. See "Identity". "Working on scripts" covers editing them without it.


pxctl
---------

	create <hub>			new tree seeded from cfg.example
	create <hub> --from DIR		new tree from an existing config dir
	create <hub> --setup		as above, then straight into PtokaX -m
	import <hub> <configdir>	into a hub that already exists
	set <hub> syncdir <path>	where push and pull read and write
	get <hub> syncdir|statedir	print a setting or the tree
	push <hub> / pull <hub>		move scripts/ and texts/ either way
	check <hub>			configured ports against what is listening
	setup <hub>			PtokaX -m, hub stopped
	reload-scripts <hub>		restart the hub's scripts
	log <hub> [-f]			journal for one hub
	backup <hub> [file]
	unit show|regenerate

create writes a TCPPorts line that does not collide with anything on the host.
import copies your settings untouched, TCPPorts included.


Working on scripts
------------------

Editing scripts in the hub's tree needs sudo for every change. Keep them
somewhere you own instead and copy them across in one command. Only scripts/ and
texts/ move. cfg/ never does, for the reason above.

	sudo pxctl set PXdev syncdir ~/.local/share/dchubs/PXdev
	pxctl push PXdev --dry-run
	sudo pxctl push PXdev

Both directions need root, because the tree does. --dry-run does not.

To avoid overwriting production data, pxctl records what was last pushed, and
prompts before overwriting anything the hub has changed since then. pull is the
reverse. --force skips the prompting. .pxignore in the syncdir keeps files out of
the diff entirely.


Lua console
-----------

Trying one API call means a push and a script reload, and both need sudo. The
console runs a chunk against a fresh Lua state with the full hub API. Output
goes to the journal.

	systemctl stop ptokax@PXdev
	systemctl enable --now ptokax-console@PXdev.socket
	systemctl start ptokax@PXdev

	socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/run/ptokax/PXdev-console.sock < chunk.lua
	journalctl PTOKAX_SUBSYSTEM=console -f

systemd refuses to start a socket whose service is already up, so the hub has
to be stopped for that middle command. It is a one time cost. From then on the
socket comes up with sockets.target at boot.

Write a chunk, close the socket. Each connection gets a new state, so globals
do not survive between chunks. Settings and registrations do.

The socket is root-only. A chunk runs as the hub user and has os.execute, so
console-access.conf.example grants more than push does.

A chunk that never returns blocks the hub the same way a script does, and only
SIGKILL ends it.


Logging
-------

Output goes to the journal. logs/ is only written when PtokaX runs outside a
service manager.

	journalctl -u ptokax@PXdev -p err
	journalctl PTOKAX_SCRIPT=welcome.lua
	journalctl PTOKAX_PORT=411

Priorities are real, so -p err and -p warning filter properly.

A hub that binds some of its ports but not all now starts and warns, rather
than being silent about it. check shows the same thing:

	ports      1209;411
	listening  Listening on 1209


Identity
--------

Each hub runs as ptokax-HubName under DynamicUser=yes and cannot read another
hub's files.

	default		transient uid, tree at 0700 root under /var/lib/private
	stable uid	add a static user, see ptokax.sysusers.example
	group access	stable uid plus group-access.conf.example, which drops
			DynamicUser. Tree moves to /var/lib/ptokax/HubName at
			0750 for the ptokax-HubName group

Back up and restore as root. With id-mapped mounts the host shows the files
owned by nobody in the first two modes.


Several hubs on one machine
---------------------------

Instances make this routine. One 411 per address, so give each hub its own
address and turn on bind only single IP.

	ip -6 addr add 2001:db8:1::10/64 dev eth0

The bound address comes from resolving HubAddress, so this holds when the
address you provide is the one you bind. Behind NAT it is not, and socket
activation covers that instead. README.systemd.
